From: Nicolas Villar <15879262+nvillar@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 14:04:49 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] Duo: a stale window focus gain hide does not undo the
 keyboard the tap asked for

A tap on an editor in the window of the other panel moves window focus on its down. When the focus event reaches the
app before the up (gate m2test4, G5.ime.B.fromA rep 1: down +0 ms, window focus +35 ms, up +43 ms), the window reports
the focus gain (startInputOrWindowGainedFocus, WINDOW_GAINED_FOCUS) with no focused editor, and the system answers
(soft input state unspecified, no editor: HIDE_UNSPECIFIED_WINDOW, ImeVisibilityStateComputer.computeState) with a
hide sent asynchronously: IInputMethodClientInvoker posts the oneway setImeVisibility(false) to its handler, and the
client applies it later from its own handler (MSG_SET_VISIBILITY), on the view's thread. The up then focuses the
editor, which asks for the keyboard; the request is served and the keyboard shows (+72 ms, ime=1008). The hide,
computed for the window's state at the focus gain, arrives after that and takes the keyboard away (+118 ms). fw 0023
only covers the other order (up before the focus event).

The correlation is explicit, by window and focus report, not by timing:
- The client already sends a per-process start input sequence number with every startInputOrWindowGainedFocus
  (IInputMethodManagerGlobalInvoker). While the two panel policy is active (sys.duode.panels.active=1), a hide that
  startInputOrWindowGainedFocusInternalLocked computes for a window focus gain (WINDOW_GAINED_FOCUS) is sent with the
  new internal oneway IInputMethodClient.setImeHiddenOnWindowFocusGain(statsToken, windowToken, startInputSeq)
  instead of setImeVisibility(false). The sequence number of the request being handled is kept in a thread local
  around startInputOrWindowGainedFocusWithResult (the locked computation runs on that thread, also when
  ZeroJankProxy offloads the request to the service handler), so internal signatures do not change. Every other hide
  (Back, settings, another window, the app) keeps setImeVisibility and is always applied; both go through the same
  invoker handler, so their order is unchanged.
- The client records each window focus gain report (both report paths: the start input of onPostWindowGainedFocus
  and the report-only call) under mH, by its start input sequence number: the reporting view root and the local order
  of the report. It also records, per root, the local order of its last served showSoftInput. A tagged hide is
  resolved to the root of the report it answers. It is dropped as obsolete if that root is no longer the current
  root, no longer has that window, or the report is no longer recorded (bounded to 32 reports): a hide for a window
  is never applied to another window. Otherwise it is dropped as stale if that root made a served show after that
  report (an older report's hide cannot be validated by a newer report). The staleness check runs on the view's
  thread right before the hide would be applied (setImeVisibilityOnInsetsController posts there when the loopers
  differ), where the root's show requests are made, so a request made before the hide runs is always seen. Logged
  as "Duo: ignoring the window focus gain hide ..." or "Duo: dropping the window focus gain hide for report N ..."
  (tag InputMethodManager); ImeTracker records them cancelled.
With the policy off the server never uses the new method and nothing changes. A hide that arrives before the show, or
a window without a show request after its focus report, is applied as before.

Not built or run on a device yet. Verified by git am -3 after the series and a javac type check (with the regenerated
IInputMethodClient stub) against the gate build's framework and services jars.

Co-authored-by: Copilot App <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
